      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A strong argument against deterrence posits that the very existence of nuclear weapons creates an inherent instability, fostering a constant, low-grade paranoia that encourages arms races and escalatory brinkmanship. They argue that the risk of accidental launch or miscalculation is too high, making perpetual tension a moral failure.\n\nTo dismantle this, you must pivot back to the necessity of the threat: \"While the risk of miscalculation is a genuine concern, the alternative—unfettered aggression by a nuclear-armed state—presents a far greater, immediate, and certain threat to human life. Deterrence is a managed risk, whereas unchecked ambition is a guaranteed disaster.\"",
      "source": "target",
